is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Router4 itself is not down as a gateway for Location C. Its LAN interface is up and answering: from PC11/PC12, 192.168.200.1 pings successfully and ARP resolves to e2:54:92:19:de:09, and on router4 eth0 is UP with 192.168.200.1/24. However, the routing subsystem on router4 is down: the FRRouting service is “inactive (dead)”. With FRR stopped, router4 still forwards for directly connected subnets (so the default gateway appears alive), but it does not actively participate in dynamic routing. As a result, inter-site reachability is inconsistent/missing. Evidence: PC11 reaches 172.16.10.4 (another site) via router4, but cannot reach 172.18.51.15 (timeout). On router4, the kernel table shows RIP-learned entries, but there is no route to 172.18.51.0/24; with FRR down these routes are stale and will not update, breaking paths to some remote networks.

Localization: router4, FRR daemon. Interfaces eth0 (192.168.200.1/24), eth4 (192.168.15.2/24), and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24) are up, but the routing process is not running.

Root cause: The FRR service on router4 is stopped, preventing dynamic routing updates and complete inter-site connectivity.

Corrective fix: Restart and re-enable FRR on router4 (systemctl start frr; systemctl enable frr), then verify RIP neighbors and routes (vtysh “show ip route”). Once FRR is running, routes to remote subnets (e.g., 172.18.51.0/24) should be restored. Also investigate why FRR was stopped to prevent recurrence.
=== END ===